Z||000000||curtis||01-27-2000||11:25 PM||||I was checking out one of the price giudes for the Fieros and one mentioned a MERA V6 in 87 and 88. Only 88 of them were made in 87, and 159 in 88. Whats the differences in the package?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierosound||01-28-2000||12:01 AM||||The Mera GTS was a rebodied Fiero built by Corporate Concepts Ltd.   They were sold through Pontiac Dealers along with the regular Fiero and carried the regular Pontiac warranty.  Personally, I think they look great!!  The obviously have a resemblance to a Ferrari, and in fact were sued over this by Ferarri.  The lawsuit was dropped when GM pulled the plug on the Fiero, as this effectively stopped Corporate Concepts production of further Meras.<P>See photos here:<BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=29019&a=173027" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=29019&a=173027</A> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000002||Tom88gt||01-28-2000||02:56 PM||||All true.  Pontiac actually purchased  Ferrari 308/328s when they began the Fireo project.  Toyota may have copied the Fiero when they made the MR2 (first the notch back, then the fast back.  Hmmm?)  But Pontiac copied the 308/328 to some extent as well when they made the Fiero.  <P>Meras has their own market value but there as so few and rarely sold that the price ranges are pure speculation.  If you find one for sell, it may be a good buy, or not.  Remember all the speculators who bought Ferraris and old muscle cars at inflated prices?  They are now bankrupt.  I would price them comparable to professionally built 308/328 kit cars.  thats all they were anyway.  <P>The C&C Mera molds were sold to another kit car company anyway and are still in use.  Actually, many kit car companies selling ferrari and Lamborguini kits are using the same molds, or making molds from someone else's molds.   ||||reg||1
